<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maria Lekapene</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 963</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Irene Lekapene</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maria Lekapene (died c. 963) was a Byzantine princess, granddaughter of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-i-lekapenos">Romanos I Lekapenos</a>, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Peter I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 927. The marriage sealed the peace that ended Simeon I's wars, by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> recognised Peter's imperial title and the Bulgarian patriarchate. She took the name Irene, 'peace', on her marriage and was the mother of Boris II and Roman.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maria-lekapene.jpg" alt="Maria Lekapene" width="320" height="137" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Cplakidas (uploader) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Peter_I_of_Bulgaria_with_Irene_Lekapene.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
